<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.9.8: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00530</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.9.8: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>de7265b8-bcd5-4c60-8381-c883daf44230</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="6a5dfa57-fef9-41e5-b8cd-c8e299fa4c13" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>1. Here is an inequality: \(\frac{7x+6}2\leq3x+2\)Select two of the values that are a solution to the inequality.</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(x = -3\), \(x = -2\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.9.1. </para><para>The correct answers are \(x = -3\), \(x = -2\).
      </para><para>Substitute the number 1 in for \(h\) to see how much it would cost to rent a scooter for 1 hour.
        \(9(1)+11 \gt 15(1)\)
        \(20\gt 15\)
        To rent a scooter for 1 hour, it costs $20 from Company A and $15 from Company B. Company A costs more than Company B.</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="c160b1e0-fda6-40eb-a8da-4e31848f0df5" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>2. Which number line is represented by the inequality \( x ≤ 6 \)?</para><para> <figure><media alt="A number line is shown. There is an open circle on 6. The number line to the right of 6 is highlighted."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/9c5dde2d476cfd6b22bf6f0d8b5acb0862ed7e79"/></media></figure></para>
        <para> <figure><media alt="A number line is shown. There is a closed circle on 6. The number line to the left of 6 is highlighted."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/0b925536b0ea0b7f58d7e36b38257ff2ac39aa91"/></media></figure></para>
        <para>  <figure><media alt="A number line is shown. There is an open circle on 6. The number line to the left of 6 is highlighted."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/8cbb7032815b55597bfb00dd315a9d9f2a6719a9"/></media></figure></para>
        <para>  <figure><media alt="A number line is shown. There is a closed circle on 6. The number line to the right of 6 is highlighted.&#10;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/6bfd8560461129262b0ae3ad730b0a1c85a9d8a3"/></media></figure></para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>A.</para><para>B.</para><para>C.</para><para>D.</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! <figure><media alt="A number line is shown. There is an open circle on 6. The number line to the left of 6 is highlighted."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/8cbb7032815b55597bfb00dd315a9d9f2a6719a9"/></media></figure></para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.9.2 </para><para>The correct answer is <figure><media alt="A number line is shown. There is an open circle on 6. The number line to the left of 6 is highlighted."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/8cbb7032815b55597bfb00dd315a9d9f2a6719a9"/></media></figure></para><para>\(x \le 6\) means that the solution would be any number less than or equal to 6.
        The graph would have a filled in circle on the number 6, because it can be equal to 6. The arrow would point to infinity on the left of the 6, because it is less than 6.</para></solution></exercise></section>
<para>For questions 3 – 4, use the following information:</para>
<para><quote>Scooter rental company A charges $9 per hour plus a $11 initiation fee. Scooter rental company B charges $15 per hour.</quote></para><section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="eadc8e80-78e7-4b3a-bf8a-ae7a81f3a6b4" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>3. A customer writes the following inequality to represent the situation: \(9h + 11 &gt; 15h\).</para><para>Which of the following is true about the given inequality?</para>
    <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>Company A costs more than Company B.</para><para>Company A costs less than Company B.</para><para>Company A costs the same as Company B.</para><para>h represents the number of scooters rented.</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! Company A costs more than Company B.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.9.3.
      </para><para>The correct answer is Company A costs more than Company B. </para><para>Substitute the number 1 in for \(h\) to see how much it would cost to rent a scooter for 1 hour.
        \(9(1)+11 \gt 15(1)\)
        \(20 \lt 5\)
        To rent a scooter for 1 hour, it costs $20 from Company A and $15 from Company B. Company A costs more than Company B.</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="7c8cc139-3f1c-48ef-aa50-44fc5400af42" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>4. For which number of hours does Company B cost less?</para><para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>1 hour</para><para>2 hours</para><para>3 hours</para><para>4 hours</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! 1 hour</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.9.3

      </para><para>The correct answer is 1 hour.
      </para><para>
        Substitute the number 1 in for \(h\) to see how much it would cost to rent a scooter for 1 hour.
        Company A: 9(1)+11=$20 
        Company B: 15(1)=$15 
        To rent a scooter for 1 hour, it costs $20 from Company A and $15 from Company B.
      </para><para>
        Substitute the number 2 in for \(h\) to see how much it would cost to rent a scooter for 2 hours.
        Company A: 
        9(2)+11=$29
        Company B: 15(2)=$30
        To rent a scooter for 2 hours, it costs $29 from Company A and $30 from Company B.
      </para><para>
        Substitute the number 3 in for \(h\) to see how much it would cost to rent a scooter for 3 hours.
        Company A: 9(3)+11=$38
        Company B: 15(3)=$45
        To rent a scooter for 3 hours, it costs $38 from Company A and $45 from Company B.
      </para><para>
        Substitute the number 4 in for \(h\) to see how much it would cost to rent a scooter for 4 hours.
        Company A: 9(4)+11=$47
        Company B: 15(4)=$60
        To rent a scooter for 4 hours, it costs $47 from Company A and $60 from Company B.
      </para><para>Company B costs the least for 1 hour.</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="41abc371-b9ff-4743-8f77-ac06b17d1353" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>For questions 5 – 7, use the following information:</para><para><quote>Cycle Rentals offers 20 membership points for each day that a customer rents a bike. Bikes Unlimited offers 5 membership points for each day that a customer rents a bike plus 60 points for joining the membership club.</quote></para><para>5. The equation \(20x = 5x + 60\) represents a possible constraint about the situation. Solve for \(x\).</para>
      <para>\(x\) = ____ </para>
    </problem><solution><para>Correct! The correct answer is 4. If you rent a bike for longer than 4 days, you earn more membership points at Cycle Rentals.</para><para>
        \(20x=5x+60\)
        \(15x=60\)
        \(x=4 \) 
      </para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.9.4. </para><para>The correct answer is 4. If you rent a bike for longer than 4 days, you earn more membership points at Cycle Rentals.</para></solution></exercise></section><section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="bc34b533-547f-47a7-942e-01f28d487514" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>6. Which shop offers more membership points if you rent a bike for longer than the constraint you found in the previous question?</para><para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>Cycle Rentals</para><para>Bikes Unlimited</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! Cycle Rentals</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.9.4. </para><para>The correct answer is Cycle Rentals.
      </para><para>
        The constraint from question 5 is \(x=4\). 
        Since a number larger than 4 is needed to determine which shop offers more membership points if you rent a bike for longer than the constraint, choose to substitute in the number 5 for \(x\) to calculate how many membership points can be earned in 5 days.
        Cycle Rentals: \(20x=20(5)=100\) membership points
        Bikes Unlimited: \(5x+60=5(5)+60=25+60=85\) membership points 
      </para></solution></exercise></section><section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="90b0f163-6e68-4323-abe1-441b5d71512c" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>7. Which number line represents the solution to the inequality, \(20x &gt; 5x + 60\), representing the situation when you earn more membership points at Cycle Rentals?</para><para><figure><media alt="A number line is shown from 2 to 6 with an open circle on the point 4. The number line to the right of 4 is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/9baf590bad3d5c61a1f40792b71b0ba0e6e5f71b"/></media></figure></para>
        <para><figure><media alt="A number line is shown from 2 to 6 with a closed circle on the point 4. The number line to the right of 4 is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/8006903b8bee6446e2bf991eedd28c997ef3d0f4"/></media></figure></para>
        <para><figure><media alt="A number line is shown from 2 to 6 with a closed circle on the point 4. The number line to the left of 4 is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/2cdbc8eae1d8ce8f1f626cbccc9d0285c406b8a1"/></media></figure></para>
        <para><figure><media alt="A number line is shown from 2 to 6 with an open circle on the point 4. The number line to the left of 4 is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/d320204ee9b51df65b814e8ddcbb82312b7809ad"/></media></figure></para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>A.</para><para>B.</para><para>C.</para><para>D.</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! <figure><media alt="A number line is shown from 2 to 6 with an open circle on the point 4. The number line to the right of 4 is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/9baf590bad3d5c61a1f40792b71b0ba0e6e5f71b"/></media></figure></para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 2.9.2 and 2.9.3 </para><para>The correct answer is<figure><media alt="A number line is shown from 2 to 6 with an open circle on the point 4. The number line to the right of 4 is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/9baf590bad3d5c61a1f40792b71b0ba0e6e5f71b"/></media></figure></para><para>
        Solve for \(x\).
        \(20x \gt 5x+60\) 
        \(15x \gt 60 \)
        \(x \gt 4\) 
        The graph will have an open circle at 4, since the solution can not be equal to 4, with the arrow pointing to infinity to the right because the solution is greater than 4.
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="cbd8f0f5-868b-4eb0-907d-8d32d19e0c12" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>8. Which number line represents the solution to \(5+8x&lt;3(2x+4)\)?</para><para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/24b00cad1e514adf5b68db96506d38fb25aaf677"/></media></figure></para>
        <para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/5e52f4f9c15fc02f77cc8e962ff6eb35698a975f"/></media></figure></para>
        <para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/a22f6aa3de031d8691fef966087b580e8c77d21c"/></media></figure></para>
        <para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/a3dfa5c4eb28bc1ab6a159329d0ad49e1847a20e"/></media></figure></para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>A.</para><para>B.</para><para>C.</para><para>D.</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! <figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/a3dfa5c4eb28bc1ab6a159329d0ad49e1847a20e"/></media></figure></para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.9.5 </para><para>The correct answer is<figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/a3dfa5c4eb28bc1ab6a159329d0ad49e1847a20e"/></media></figure></para><para>
        Solve for \(x\).
        \(5+8x \lt 3(2x+4)\)
        \(5+8x \lt 6x+12\)
        \(5+2x \lt 12\)
        \(2x \lt 7\) 
        \(x \lt \frac72\) 
        \(x \lt 3\frac12\)
        The graph will have an open circle at \(x \lt 3\frac12\), since the solution can not be equal to \(3 \frac12\), with the arrow pointing to infinity to the left because the solution is less than \(3\frac12\).
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="865a1d9b-7090-4ea4-97a2-8957c4880e3f" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>9. Find the solution set to this inequality by solving the related equation: \(2x-3&gt;\frac{2x-5}2\)</para><para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(x&lt;\frac12\)</para><para>\(x&gt;\frac12\)</para><para>\(x\leq\frac12\)</para><para>\(x\geq\frac12\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(x&gt;\frac12\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.9.5 </para><para>The correct answer is \(x&gt;\frac12\). </para><para>
        Solve for \(x\).
        \(2x−3\gt\frac{2x-5}{2}\)
        \(4x−6\gt 2x-5\)
        \(2x−6\gt -5\)
        \(2x\gt 1\)
        \(x\gt\frac12 \)
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="74ac8378-e680-406b-8f15-d884098ae579" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>10. Here is an inequality: \(\frac{-10+x}4+5\geq\frac{7x-5}3\). What value of \(x\) will produce equality (or make the two sides equal)?</para><para>\(x\) = ____ </para>
    </problem><solution><para>Correct! 2</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.9.4.
      </para><para>The correct answer is 2.
      </para><para>
        Solve for \(x\).
        \(\frac{-10+x}{4}+5=\frac{7x-5}{3}\)
        \(12(\frac{-10+x}{4}+5=\frac{7x-5}{3})\) 
        \(3(-10+x)+(12)(5)=4(7x-5)\)
      </para><para>
        \(\begin{align*}
        -30+3x+60&amp;=28x-20 \\
        3x+30&amp;=28x-20\\
        3x+50&amp;=28x\\
        50&amp;=25x\\
        2&amp;=x\\
        \end{align*}\)
        The solution 2 will produce equality. It will make both sides equal.
      </para><para>
        \(\frac{-10+2}{4}+5\)
        \(\frac{-8}{4}+5\)
        \((-2+5)\)
        3
      </para><para>
        \(\frac{(7)(2)-5}{3}\)
        \(\frac{14-5}{3}\)
        \(\frac{9}{3}\)
        3
      </para></solution></exercise></section></section>
</content>
